---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RuleOf72Calculator from '@/components/tools/calc/RuleOf72Calculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="rule-of-72-calculator">
  <RuleOf72Calculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the rule of 72 calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The rule of 72 estimates how many years money takes to double: divide 72 by the yearly rate in
      percent. The rule of 72 calculator also gives the exact answer and two other rules, so you can see
      the error.
    </p>
    <p>
      The exact time for yearly compounding is <code>t = ln 2 ÷ ln(1 + r)</code>, where <code>r</code> is
      the rate as a decimal. The rule of 70 is <code>70 ÷ r</code>. With continuous compounding the exact
      time is <code>100 × ln 2 ÷ r = 69.31 ÷ r</code>, which is the rule of 69.3.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      At 8% a year, the rule of 72 gives 72 ÷ 8 = 9 years. The exact time is 9.006 years, so the rule is
      0.006 years short. The rule of 70 gives 8.75 years and the rule of 69.3 gives 8.66 years. At 2%, the
      rule of 72 gives 36 years against an exact 35.00, and the rule of 70 gives 35. At 20%, the rule of 72
      gives 3.6 years against an exact 3.80.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The rate is the same every year and the growth stays invested. Tax, fees and new deposits are not
      included. The same rules work for anything that grows by a fixed share each year, such as prices
      under steady inflation.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
